 "vyzhimka": {
  "tekst": "I found a real-world identity behind Business Intelligence DNA, but it appears to be a very new business/brand, so I would distinguish “legitimate legal/business presence” from “established track record.”\n\nThe site identifies the operator as Wealthboosterpro LLC, doing business as “Business Intelligence DNA,” with an address at 5830 E 2nd St, Suite 7000, Casper, Wyoming. More importantly, its terms identify the two people actually providing the consulting as Andrii Zhyla and Maryna Zhyla: Andrii handles strategy/positioning, and Maryna handles business economics and is described as a former CFO. ([businessinteldna.com](https://businessinteldna.com/))\n\nThere is independent evidence that these are real people with a business history. Maryna Zhyla has a LinkedIn profile in the Los Angeles area listing Wealthboosterpro Corp. and describing a long entrepreneurial background. ([linkedin.com](https://www.linkedin.com/in/marinazhyla?utm_source=openai)) Andrii Zhyla likewise has an established LinkedIn presence in Los Angeles and links to wealthboosterpro.com; his profile describes him as an entrepreneur/finance professional. ([linkedin.com](https://www.linkedin.com/in/azhyla?utm_source=openai)) There are also older corporate records connecting Andrii and Maryna Zhyla to another U.S. company, A.M. Bizz LLC, and Andrii appears in UK corporate records as a former person with significant control of J.B. Finance LLP. ([florida.intercreditreport.com](https://florida.intercreditreport.com/company/a-m-bizz-llc-l18000151698?utm_source=openai)) Separately, California corporate-record data shows an Andrii Zhyla as CEO/CFO/director of an active California company, DA Films Corp. ([bizprofile.net](https://www.bizprofile.net/ca/santa-monica/da-films-corp?utm_source=openai))\n\nThere are nevertheless some reasons I would exercise normal due diligence before paying them a substantial amount:\n\n- Business Intelligence DNA is extremely new. Search-engine domain-registration data indicates businessinteldna.com appeared among newly registered .com domains around September 6, 2026, and the site's first terms took effect September 8, 2026. ([com.all-url.info](https://com.all-url.info/21/25/?utm_source=openai)) So you're not looking at a consultancy with years of operating history under this particular name.\n- The Wyoming address isn't evidence of a physical Business Intelligence DNA office. 5830 E 2nd St, Suite 7000 is used by Republic Registered Agent LLC for numerous unrelated Wyoming companies. Official Wyoming filings reproduced by the SEC show Republic Registered Agent at exactly that address. ([sec.gov](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1945868/000194586823000006/exhibit_d_23.pdf?utm_source=openai)) In other words, this is essentially a registered-agent/business-registration address rather than proof that the Zhylas operate out of Casper.\n- I found very little independent customer history for “Business Intelligence DNA” itself. Given how recently the domain and brand appeared, that's not particularly surprising, but it means testimonials, reputation and delivery history under this name can't yet provide much verification.\n- On the positive side, the website is unusually specific about who provides the work, pricing, contractual terms, refunds, data processing and third-party providers. The terms disclose a $500 diagnostic service, implementation starting at $5,000, and separate AI-agent pricing, along with limitations and a defined refund mechanism. ([businessinteldna.com](https://businessinteldna.com/terms)) The privacy policy also explicitly discloses that the business consists of Andrii and Maryna with no employees and identifies services such as Anthropic, Google, ElevenLabs, Twilio, Stripe and Netlify that process customer information. ([businessinteldna.com](https://businessinteldna.com/privacy)) That level of disclosure is a positive signal, although naturally the company's own statements aren't independent verification.\n\nSo my assessment is: I don't see evidence that Business Intelligence DNA is an anonymous scam operation. There are identifiable people behind it with a verifiable business history, and the website provides substantive contractual and privacy information. However, Business Intelligence DNA itself appears to have launched only in September 2026, meaning it currently lacks the independent history I'd want before treating it as an established consultancy.\n\nIf you're considering paying the $500 diagnostic fee, that looks like a relatively bounded way of evaluating them. If you're considering the $5,000+ implementation, I'd do additional verification first: confirm Wealthboosterpro LLC directly in Wyoming's corporate registry, establish exactly who owns/manages it, check the Zhylas' claimed professional/CFO history, look for litigation/complaints, verify prior projects and clients, and examine whether their AI-service claims are technically credible.\n\nIf you want, I can do that deeper background check on Andrii Zhyla, Maryna Zhyla and Wealthboosterpro LLC—including corporate records, prior companies, litigation, complaints, and their professional histories—and give you a risk assessment.",
